id: pm-5kfzjh
title: "Managed npm update checks report a subprocess timeout as npm_registry_lookup_failed, and the fixed 10 s npm view budget makes the Windows nightly extension-freshness test flaky"
description: "Nightly run 44087418672 (GitHub alert issue 1747, windows-latest Node 24 shard 4/4) failed tests/integration/extensions/extension-diagnostic-purity.integration.spec.ts in both the initial attempt and the retry (21.6 s for two attempts) at its first registry-backed freshness check: update_check_status was failed instead of checked. The test passes in 5.3 s on green Windows nights (run 43867277935) while spawning npm view several times, so each failing attempt spent the full GITHUB_UPDATE_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS (10 s) on one cold npm view through the Windows command shell. runNpmUpdateQuery in src/sdk/extension/update-check.ts maps every failure, including that timeout, to the stable code npm_registry_lookup_failed, so neither the nightly log nor a user's pm extension --manage output can tell a slow npm from an unavailable registry. Proposed: report a distinct stable code for a timed-out lookup (killed subprocess with ETIMEDOUT or SIGTERM) without echoing npm's command, stderr or environment, name the budget in the diagnostic, and give the integration test a deterministic registry check budget so Windows cold npm starts do not fail it. Related evidence work: pm-q7c36n kept subprocess timeout and signal evidence for registry install acceptance; pm-2zjs0g measures nightly family rates."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[7]: diagnostics,extensions,flaky-test,"gh:1747",nightly,update-check,windows
created_at: "2026-11-17T21:58:53.463Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-18T20:32:53.923Z"
closed_at: "2026-11-18T17:21:06.188Z"
completed_at: "2026-11-18T17:21:06.188Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 60
acceptance_criteria: "A timed-out npm update lookup reports a distinct stable code (no npm command, stderr or environment in the output) and the diagnostic names the budget; the extension-diagnostic-purity integration test no longer depends on a cold Windows npm start finishing inside 10 s; unit tests cover the timeout and failure classifications"
parent: pm-doxj
risk: low
confidence: medium
severity: low
resolution: "Update lookups run under runBoundedUpdateQuery. It spawns the provider attached to pm's process group and console (stdin and stderr ignored, stdout capped: npm 64 KiB, git 1 MiB), so a terminal Ctrl-C reaches it with pm, and no signal handlers are installed. A monotonic deadline settles each lookup without waiting for its pipes. Every outcome (timeout, overflow, failed spawn) is classified first, and the best-effort teardown then starts on a later turn of the event loop, in createProviderTeardown and src/core/shared/process-tree.ts. The teardown stops a still-running launcher and its live descendants: System32 taskkill /T on Windows, resolved only from a fully qualified SystemRoot or windir; elsewhere, the system ps (/bin/ps or /usr/bin/ps, fixed environment), used only in this PID namespace (NSpid evidence), with each descendant's parent re-checked in /proc. The root is always killed through its ChildProcess handle, never by a possibly reused PID. Helpers and the provider exit wait are bounded to 2 s and unref'd when they expire. A lookup still running at its budget reports npm_registry_lookup_timed_out with update_check_timeout_ms; the summary carries the budget only on a failed check, and a stored timed-out record has unknown availability. Other provider execution failures report npm_registry_lookup_failed, and output validation keeps its own codes. PM_EXTENSION_UPDATE_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS sets the budget (default 10000, clamped to 1000-25000, below the 30 s extension state lock wait). manage --fix-managed-state keeps the 10000 default and releases provider permits as each lookup settles, so its locked scan takes no longer than on main; elsewhere, permits are held through teardown, so at most four providers are alive. The GH-1747 purity test is split into four scenarios that each fit CI's default 30 s limit: three registry-backed checks, each with the 25 s budget, and a stalled npm fixture."
expected_result: "A slow npm view is reported as a timeout distinct from an unavailable registry, and the Windows nightly freshness test is deterministic"
actual_result: "Unit tests cover fake-timer and monotonic deadlines, a clean exit after the deadline, a failed exit while a descendant holds the pipes, stdio-less spawns, deferred teardown ordering, permit accounting, exit-wait bounds and unref, and the process-tree module end to end with mocked spawn, process.kill, fs and platform. Real-process POSIX tests show a stalled launcher settles in about 1 s, a live launcher and its descendant are killed at the deadline, a Ctrl-C to the host's process group stops the lookup with the host, and stderr never reaches the status. Negative controls fail without the fixes. The extension-diagnostic-purity scenarios pass with real npm and a stalled npm fixture. The full local suite passes (13377 tests)."
component: extensions
