comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-18T10:27:28.957Z","harness:claude-code","Implemented on branch feat/sdk-stable-consumer-classifications. runNpmUpdateQuery now runs under runBoundedUpdateQuery's explicit deadline. A lookup still running when the budget passes reports npm_registry_lookup_timed_out, and every other failure reports npm_registry_lookup_failed. checkNpmUpdate carries timeout_ms; the managed record and the extension summary carry it as update_check_timeout_ms, present only on a timeout. PM_EXTENSION_UPDATE_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS sets the budget for both providers (resolveExtensionUpdateCheckTimeoutMs: integer ms, default 10000, clamped to 1000-120000, and the default for a value that is not finite). The GH-1747 integration test (extension-diagnostic-purity) gives registry-backed checks a 60 s budget, so a cold Windows npm start no longer fails it. It also proves the timeout path end to end: a local registry that answers after 3 s under a 1 s budget reports npm_registry_lookup_timed_out with update_check_timeout_ms 1000. The local run is green in 2.5 s.\n"
notes[35]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-18T10:27:13.785Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on f0cbdfc88). The update-check findings and how each was fixed:\n- P2: the timeout was inferred from execFile's rejection (killed, and not a maxBuffer overflow). A provider that handles SIGTERM and exits 0 would resolve with partial output. Fix: runBoundedUpdateQuery owns the deadline, a setTimeout that aborts the child through an AbortSignal. A lookup still running when the deadline passes is reported as *_timed_out even when the child then exits cleanly. isExecTimeout and execFile's timeout option are gone. A unit test makes the child exit 0 with partial output on abort.\n- P2: the behaviour change had no contract_change declaration. Fix: declared on pm-5kfzjh and pm-43p2c3.\n- P3: the summary always carried an update_check_timeout_ms key, as undefined. Fix: the refresh, the normalizer and the summary add the key only when a lookup timed out (withUpdateCheckTimeout, optionalTimeoutBudget). Tests assert the key is absent with not.toHaveProperty.\n- P3: the default-budget tests inherited a PM_EXTENSION_UPDATE_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS exported by the shell. Fix: they stub it unset and restore it.\n- P3: the docs overstated GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0. Fix: git also runs with GCM_INTERACTIVE=never, and the docs say that another askpass program or credential helper that prompts can still hold the lookup until its budget runs out.\nAbort semantics on Windows: an execFile abort emits an error that destroys the child's stdio and settles at once. It does not wait for a grandchild still holding the pipe, which the timeout option did with shell: true."
  "2026-11-18T10:47:18.439Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 2 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on fd842f55f). The update-check findings and how each was fixed:\n- P2: the AbortSignal deadline still waited for the pipes. Node drops the abort listener when the launcher exits, so a descendant holding stdout or stderr kept execFile pending; Codex measured 1.38 s for a 400 ms budget. Aborting also killed only the launcher. Fix: runBoundedUpdateQuery now uses spawn directly, because execFile does not forward detached. The child is spawned detached on POSIX (its own process group) with windowsHide, stdin ignored and stderr drained and discarded, and stdout is bounded by a per-provider maxBuffer (npm 64 KiB, git 1 MiB). The deadline settles the promise on its own, destroys the pipes and calls killProcessTree: SIGKILL to the process group on POSIX, taskkill /T /F on Windows. killProcessTree moved from src/sdk/test/execution.ts into src/core/shared/process-tree.ts so both callers share it. The new tests/integration/extensions/update-check-process-tree.integration.spec.ts (POSIX) runs a fake git that leaves sleep 30 on its pipes and exits 0. The check settles as github_remote_lookup_timed_out in about 1 s, and the descendant is gone within 5 s. On the round-1 design this test would wait the full 30 s.\n- P3: a stale stored update_check_timeout_ms survived normalization next to a non-timeout reason. Fix: the managed-state normalizer keeps it only beside npm_registry_lookup_timed_out or github_remote_lookup_timed_out. The codes are spelled locally because importing update-check would close a cycle through install-sources and bundled-catalog. Tests pin both codes and the drop cases."
  "2026-11-18T10:52:32.932Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1752 bot review, first pass: CodeRabbit on fd842f55f. The update-check findings and how each was handled:\n- Major (outside the diff): the deadline did not settle while a helper held the pipes. Already fixed in c564fc892 (spawn plus an independent deadline). The Minor about only the direct child being signalled was fixed in the same commit by the process-group or taskkill /T teardown, with a real fake-git integration test.\n- A stale stored budget passed normalization. Already fixed in c564fc892: the budget is kept only beside a timed-out reason.\n- The snapshot acknowledgement said only required mappings were affected. Fix: it now also names consumers that assert the exact keyof ManagedExtensionRecord key set.\n- The integration timeout scenario could pass before npm reached the registry, and three 60 s lookups did not fit the 120 s test timeout. Fix: the registry now holds the request unanswered, the budget is 8 s so a warm npm has time to reach it, the test asserts the held request arrived (heldRequests is 1), and the test timeout is 300 s.\n- Two unit assertions could pass if an entry were dropped. Fix: they now assert the offline refresh keeps both entries and normalization keeps the entry's identity.\n- Declined here, filed as pm-9x9drr: manage --fix-managed-state holds the extension state lock across the remote lookups. That predates this change, and default behaviour is unchanged. Fixing it means splitting the locked manage handler."
  "2026-11-18T11:09:39.155Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 3 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 5af18de6d). Each finding and how it was handled:\n- P2: on POSIX the detached lookup group survived Ctrl-C, because it is outside the terminal's foreground process group. Fix: trackDetachedProcessGroup in src/core/shared/process-tree.ts adds listeners only while a lookup is in flight. On exit it kills the tracked groups. On SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP it kills them, removes itself, and re-raises the signal when no other listener remains, so the default exit still applies. A real-process integration test launches the built SDK with a stalled fake git and sends the host SIGINT. The host exits with SIGINT, and the launcher and its descendant are both gone. The negative control (tracking disabled in dist) fails that test.\n- P2: when taskkill could not start or failed, nothing was killed. Fix: killProcessTree falls back to killing the root process. runBoundedUpdateQuery also kills a direct child that is still running after the tree teardown, including when the teardown rejects.\n- P2: the lock-scope issue again. Now fixed in this PR as pm-9x9drr.\n- P2: the 8 s held-registry case brought back the npm start-up dependency. Fix: the purity test's timeout case now runs a stalled npm fixture (sh on POSIX, npm.cmd on Windows) under a 2 s budget, so start-up time no longer decides the outcome.\n- P3: an unreaped zombie descendant failed the liveness probe. Fix: the probe treats a process that is gone, or shown by ps with state Z, as stopped.\n- P3: the contract_change omitted the keyof ManagedExtensionRecord migration. Fix: before, after and migrate now cover it."
  "2026-11-18T11:25:35.267Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 4 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 67c7eef6b). Each finding and how it was handled:\n- P2: a host that registered process.once(\"SIGTERM\", shutdown) before a lookup had that listener removed before the cleanup counted listeners. The cleanup saw none and re-raised SIGTERM, cutting the host's async shutdown short. Fix: the cleanup is prepended (prependListener), so it runs first, while the host's once wrapper is still registered, and re-raises only when it is the only listener. A real-process test registers process.once SIGTERM in a host running the built SDK: the host finishes its 200 ms shutdown, writes its marker, exits 0, and the lookup group is gone. The negative control (an appended listener) fails that test.\n- P2: freshness mismatch after the lock release, and raw filesystem errors outside the lock's host-error boundary. Both come from the pm-9x9drr lock move, which is reverted out of this PR. pm-9x9drr is open again and records both as design requirements.\n- P2: closure and changelog belong in this branch. Done in the same commit: escape class, gate evidence and close evidence on pm-gh1749, pm-5kfzjh and pm-43p2c3, then pnpm changelog:pm.\n- P3: the interruption test could leave its host subprocess running if setup failed. Fix: interruptHost kills and awaits the host in a finally block."
  "2026-11-18T11:44:14.119Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 5 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 52d233ceb), and a design change:\n- P1: the surface snapshot still recorded the old Decision & ({} | null) default type. Fix: regenerated from main's snapshot. It now records NonNullable<Decision> | Extract<Decision, null> as additive, with the single ManagedExtensionRecord acknowledgement.\n- P2 (two findings): a host prependOnceListener added after a lookup starts was still cut off by the re-raise, and a lookup started in a worker thread was detached while its cleanup listeners sat in the worker, which process signals never reach. These are the third and fourth round in which signal handling broke a host. Every listener-ordering rule had another counter-case.\n- Decision: stop detaching. Lookups stay in pm's process group, so a terminal Ctrl-C reaches them exactly as before, and pm installs no signal handlers. trackDetachedProcessGroup, its listeners and its unit spec are removed. For a child that is not a group leader, killProcessTree now walks the child's live descendants from one ps snapshot and kills them, then the child. On Windows it is still taskkill /T, with the root-kill fallback. A descendant whose launcher already exited is not reached on either platform, but the deadline already settles the lookup, so it no longer delays the result. The docs say exactly this.\n- Tests (POSIX, real processes): a launcher that exits while sleep holds its pipes settles in about 1 s. A still-running launcher and its descendant are both killed at the deadline. A host in its own process group, given SIGINT to the whole group as a terminal Ctrl-C would, exits with SIGINT, and its launcher and foreground helper stop. That fixture uses a foreground helper, because a non-interactive shell's background job ignores SIGINT by POSIX rule.\n- The pm-5kfzjh resolution, actual result and gate evidence are corrected to this final design."
  "2026-11-18T12:01:13.652Z","harness:claude-code","Codex round 6 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on e115066c4) and CodeRabbit's second pass (on 52d233ceb). The update-check findings and how each was handled:\n- With windowsHide and no inherited stdio, Node starts the provider without a console, so on Windows a Ctrl-C did not reach it. Fix: windowsHide is dropped for the lookup, as the original execFile path never set it.\n- The ps snapshot ran synchronously after the timeout and delayed delivering it. Fix: killProcessTree now runs ps through spawn, bounded to 10 s, so the rejection is delivered first and host timers are not blocked.\n- PID reuse: a launcher that already exited could be torn down by its recycled PID, and on Windows taskkill took a PID that might have been reused. Fix: stop() tears down by PID only while Node still reports the launcher running. Otherwise it only destroys stdout, and the root is always killed through its ChildProcess handle via killProcessTree's killRoot callback.\n- ps -o pid=,ppid= prints only the PID column on FreeBSD. Fix: -o pid= -o ppid=.\n- The ps parsing sat inside the c8 ignore block. Fix: descendantsFromProcessTable is a pure, exported function covered by tests/unit/core/shared/process-tree.spec.ts (deepest-first order, absent root, malformed rows, cycles, re-parented helpers).\n- CodeRabbit: taskkill was resolved by name, through the working directory or PATH. Fix: the absolute System32 path from SystemRoot. Its run is bounded to 10 s, and the root is killed if it fails or stalls.\n- CodeRabbit: a piped stderr held by a descendant delayed close. Fix: stdio is ignore, pipe, ignore.\n- CodeRabbit: a git exit code of 128 with a descendant holding stdout waited for the deadline and reported a timeout. Fix: a failed exit settles at once as *_lookup_failed and destroys stdout. A clean exit still waits for close, so the output is complete.\n- CodeRabbit: test strictness. The no-timeout summary now asserts the key is absent, the ps probe fails loudly when ps cannot run, and the Ctrl-C test bounds its exit wait inside try so the finally block always reaps the host.\n- CodeRabbit: the git prompt wording now says the prompts are suppressed, not that the lookup fails at once.\n- Declined: a Windows Job Object, so taskkill could reach a descendant whose launcher already exited. Node exposes no job-object API, and the limitation is documented. The guarantee that matters, a deadline that never waits for such a descendant, holds.\n- Obsolete: CodeRabbit's interruption-scope and Windows host-exit comments, because e115066c4 removed the signal handling."
  "2026-11-18T12:16:42.060Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 7 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on a8a27f03f). Each finding and how it was fixed:\n- P2: when spawn runs out of descriptors (EMFILE), it can return a child without stdout and emit error on the next tick. The runner touched stdout before registering its error listener, so that error could crash the host. Fix: runBoundedUpdateQuery and runTeardownHelper register the error listener first and settle as failed when there is no stdout. A unit test spawns a stdout-less child and then emits EMFILE.\n- P2: the teardown helpers could stall without a close event. Fix: runTeardownHelper has its own hard 10 s deadline, after which it resolves without a result, destroys stdout and SIGKILLs the helper. Spawn's timeout option is no longer used.\n- P2: PID reuse while the asynchronous ps ran. Fix: killProcessTree takes a ProcessTreeRoot { kill, alive }. The runner passes alive = Node has not reported the child's exit. An unreaped child's PID cannot be reused: libuv reaps it and Node sets exitCode in the same callback, and on Windows the open process handle pins the PID. The snapshot is used only if alive() still holds after ps returns; otherwise no descendant is signalled and the root is killed through its handle.\n- process-tree.ts is no longer excluded from coverage. tests/unit/core/shared/process-tree.spec.ts mocks spawn, process.kill and the platform, and covers the parser, the helper (success, spawn throw, error, no stdout, stall), the group signal, the descendant walk, the discarded snapshot, the ps failure, the System32 taskkill path including its SystemRoot fallback, and the root-kill fallback."
  "2026-11-18T12:31:47.746Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 8 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on df7e6ce04). Each finding and how it was handled:\n- P2: ps was resolved through PATH, yet its output decides which processes get SIGKILL. Fix: resolveTrustedPs uses /bin/ps or /usr/bin/ps only. The snapshot is used only when ps exits 0; otherwise only the root is stopped. Unit tests cover ps not installed and ps exiting with an error.\n- P2: the lock scope, raised a third time. Mitigation here: PM_EXTENSION_UPDATE_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS is clamped to at most 25000 ms, below the extension state lock's 30-second wait (120 attempts of 250 ms), so one lookup run under that lock cannot outlast a concurrent package action. That is the regression the raised budget introduced. Several lookup batches under the lock is pre-existing and is pm-9x9drr's structural fix. The docs, contract_change and resolution say 1000-25000.\n- P3, declined with reason: a descendant could exit and have its PID reused in the microseconds between the ps snapshot and the signal. PID-based signalling cannot rule that out, and the only alternatives are OS containment (a Linux pidfd per descendant, a Windows job object) that Node does not expose for descendants. Reuse within that window requires the PID space to wrap around. The killProcessTree doc comment states this limit.\n- P3: a stale stored budget on a skipped (local or built-in) record was copied into the summary. Fix: buildInstalledExtensionSummary emits update_check_timeout_ms only when the resolved status is failed. extension-command.spec.ts asserts it is absent for skipped_non_github and present for a failed timeout."
  "2026-11-18T12:45:22.507Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 9 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on c86dc7aec). Each finding and how it was handled:\n- P2: the 25 s cap bounded each lookup but not the whole scan under the lock. Five slow providers make two batches, about 50 s, against the 30 s lock wait. Fix: refreshManagedExtensionUpdates, checkNpmUpdate and checkGithubUpdate take an explicit timeoutMs, and manage --fix-managed-state passes EXTENSION_UPDATE_CHECK_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT_MS, so the lookups it runs under the lock behave exactly as on main. The override applies everywhere else. The integration test runs --fix-managed-state with the override at 2000 and asserts update_check_timeout_ms 10000. The docs and contract_change say so.\n- P2: when the host's event loop was blocked across the deadline, a queued exit or close could run before the overdue timer and be reported as an ordinary result. Fix: the runner records a monotonic deadline with performance.now(), and settle reports *_timed_out for any outcome processed at or after it. A unit test advances performance.now past the budget before the child closes cleanly and expects a timeout.\n- P2, declined with reason: on Windows the root could exit and have its PID reused in the moment before taskkill opens it. Only a job object, which Node does not expose, would close that window; the root is checked as unreaped immediately before taskkill starts. The killProcessTree doc comment states the limit."
  "2026-11-18T13:21:14.588Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit's third full pass (on c86dc7aec): 12 minor findings, all fixed.\n- Teardown identity: the Windows taskkill fallback now kills the root only if root.alive() still holds after taskkill fails, so a root that exited during taskkill is not killed by a possibly reused PID. The linked-test runner (src/sdk/test/execution.ts) now passes killProcessTree a root bound to its ChildProcess, as update-check does.\n- Tests:\n  - The POSIX ps-pending test flips alive() to false inside the fake ps, so checking alive() before ps started would fail it.\n  - The update-check teardown test records alive() and asserts it after the lookup.\n  - The liveness probe accepts only ps status 1 with no row as gone; anything else throws.\n  - The live-tree fixture waits for its single recorded sleep instead of starting a second one.\n  - The Ctrl-C host test listens for error, settles on close, bounds its cleanup wait, and records each PID as soon as it is read.\n- pm-43p2c3: a real fake git writes a credential-shaped marker to stderr and exits 128. The test asserts github_remote_lookup_failed and that the marker is absent. Its gate evidence cites this test.\n- Docs: descendant teardown is described as best effort, and the timeout result never depends on it. pm-9x9drr's description now states the current 25 s cap and the interim mitigation, and has a reproduction comment.\n- Runtime entrypoint (declined): ./sdk/runtime has never exported the mutation guard. The PR description, which wrongly claimed it did, is corrected.\n- The local full coverage run of the round-9 code had 3 timing failures under coverage and host load: scale-benchmark and deps-command hit the 30 s timeout, and the secret-guard linear-scan bound measured 5228 ms against 5000. All three pass when run on their own. They also passed in the hosted coverage run on e115066c4, so they are not regressions."
  "2026-11-18T13:35:05.974Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 10 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on a486e4799). Each finding and how it was fixed:\n- P2: stop() destroyed stdout before the ps snapshot, so a provider still writing could take SIGPIPE, exit and orphan its helpers before ps listed them. Fix: at the deadline the runner stops collecting (data after settle is discarded), keeps stdout open while killProcessTree captures and kills the tree, and closes the pipe only afterwards. A unit test holds the teardown open and asserts that stdout stays open and that overflow data cannot start a second teardown.\n- P2: refreshManagedExtensionUpdates freed a worker slot as soon as a lookup timed out, while the teardown might still be running, so more than four providers could be alive. Fix: UpdateQueryError carries its teardown promise, failedUpdateStatus records it in a WeakMap (off the serialisable status), and updateCheckTeardown(status) lets the refresh worker hold its slot until the teardown finishes. A direct checkNpmUpdate or checkGithubUpdate still returns at the deadline. A unit test holds the teardown and asserts the refresh does not complete until it is released.\n- P3: procps rejects -A under PS_PERSONALITY=bsd or CMD_ENV=bsd. Fix: the ps snapshot runs with a fixed environment, PATH=/usr/bin:/bin and LC_ALL=C. A unit test asserts that environment, and that taskkill keeps the inherited one."
  "2026-11-18T13:45:23.051Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 11 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 29a0847a4). Each finding and how it was fixed:\n- P2: the round-10 slot retention made pm package manage wait for each batch's teardown, up to 10 s, before returning its results. Under --fix-managed-state that also kept the state lock. Fix: refreshManagedExtensionUpdates separates the two. A counting semaphore (createPermits, limit 4) is acquired before each lookup and released only when updateCheckTeardown(status) settles, so no more than four providers are ever alive. The worker itself returns its entry as soon as the lookup settles, so results, and the lock, never wait for a teardown. Unit test: with five stalled providers the fifth starts only after a teardown releases its permit, and the refresh resolves while four teardowns are still pending.\n- P2: an empty or relative SystemRoot made the taskkill path relative. Fix: resolveWindowsSystemRoot accepts SystemRoot, then windir, only as a fully qualified drive path (drive letter, colon, separator), and path.win32.join builds the System32 path. Without one, taskkill is never run and the root is killed through its handle if still alive. Unit tests cover the accepted and rejected forms, the windir fallback, and the no-directory path."
  "2026-11-18T13:57:21.205Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 12 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 69dc1bc4e). Each finding and how it was handled:\n- P2: descendantsFromProcessTable copied a parent's child array for every row and shifted its queue, so 30,000 sibling rows blocked the event loop for about 3 s. Fix: children are appended in place and the queue is walked by index. A unit test parses 30,000 siblings in under a second.\n- P2: holding a provider permit through teardown could lengthen the locked --fix-managed-state scan. With five to eight stalled providers, the second batch waited up to 10 s for the first batch's teardowns. Fix: the teardown helper deadline is 2 s (ps and taskkill normally finish in milliseconds), so two batches take at most about 22 s, below the 30 s lock wait, against 20 s on main. Moving the refresh out of the lock remains pm-9x9drr.\n- P3: the purity test's 300 s override weakened CI's 30 s per-test policy. Fix: it is split. The real-npm scenarios keep a bounded 90 s override (three lookups at the 25 s budget a cold Windows runner may need). The stalled-provider and --fix-managed-state scenarios run in the default 30 s.\n- P3: test cleanup could SIGKILL a recycled PID. Fix: killQuietly signals a recorded PID only while ps reports a fixture command (sleep, sh or git) for it."
  "2026-11-18T14:05:30.055Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit's fourth full pass (on 29a0847a4), update-check findings:\n- The permit was released when SIGKILL was requested, not when the provider exited. Fix: the teardown now waits for Node's exit event, bounded to 2 s if the kill never takes effect, so a fifth provider can never start beside four still alive. Unit test: a provider whose kill never takes effect holds its teardown for exactly 2 s.\n- Already fixed in 69dc1bc4e and b176167f9: teardown outside the lock's critical path (results never wait for teardown; helpers are bounded to 2 s) and the five-entry queued-lookup test.\n- Major, declined with the documented reason: a descendant listed by ps could exit and have its PID reused before the signal. Node exposes no per-descendant handle (pidfd or job object), reuse within that window needs the PID space to wrap, and killProcessTree's doc comment states the limit.\n- Docs: a still-running provider is stopped on a best-effort basis; a provider that exited before the deadline has nothing to kill.\n- Records: pm-5kfzjh's resolution and contract migrate line state the --fix-managed-state exception. pm-9x9drr's title and actual result describe the remaining issue.\n- Tests:\n  - the stalled npm fixture records each invocation, and the test asserts two runs;\n  - the liveness probe treats only ESRCH as absence;\n  - exitWithin clears its timer;\n  - fixture pid paths reach the scripts as environment data;\n  - an exited launcher's pid is never signalled in cleanup;\n  - the root is asserted to be killed after its descendants;\n  - the Windows root exits while taskkill runs;\n  - the signal from treeRoot.kill() itself is recorded;\n  - a failed spawn has no PID and needs no tree teardown;\n  - performance.now spies are restored."
  "2026-11-18T14:19:45.678Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 13 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 273da38dd). How each finding was handled:\n- P2, declined with reason: a provider stuck in uninterruptible I/O can ignore SIGKILL beyond the 2 s exit wait, and its permit is then released while it is still alive. Never releasing until a real exit would let a permanently stuck provider (or four of them) hang pm package manage, which breaks the documented bounded timeout result. Deferring later lookups would need a new reason code, a contract change for a kernel or storage fault, not a provider behaviour. The current overshoot is bounded: every extra live provider has already been sent SIGKILL, and there are at most as many as there are managed packages.\n- P3: test cleanup relied on the command name, so a recycled PID running sleep, sh or git could be killed. Fix: readPids captures each fixture process's start time (ps -o lstart=, with a fixed environment), and killQuietly signals a pid only while its current start time equals the captured one."
  "2026-11-18T14:32:08.913Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 14 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 10e9b32fc): one P3, test-only. Start times sampled after teardown, and equal within one second, still could not prove a recorded fixture PID belonged to the fixture. Fix: the integration tests no longer signal recorded PIDs at all. Every fixture process is sleep 30 and ends on its own. The Ctrl-C test's cleanup signals only the host's own process group, and only while Node has not reaped the host, so the group id cannot have been reused. Codex found nothing beyond P3 test hygiene in this round; the P2 on stuck providers stays declined with the reason recorded in round 13."
  "2026-11-18T14:43:18.501Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 15 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 7de20b5c0). How each finding was handled:\n- P2: on the locked --fix-managed-state path, the next batch waited for the previous batch's teardowns while the state lock was held. Fix: refreshManagedExtensionUpdates takes holdPermitsThroughTeardown (default true), and the locked manage path passes false. There, a permit is released as soon as its lookup settles, exactly as on main, so the locked scan takes no longer than main's. Unlocked callers still bound live providers through teardown. Unit test: with false, the fifth lookup starts while four teardowns are pending.\n- P3: a spawn that failed without creating a process (no PID) waited 2 s for an exit that cannot come. Fix: teardown finishes at once without a PID. The unit test asserts the teardown resolves with no timers advanced.\n- P3, declined: an unannotated inline policy that mixes decision types, such as { default: null, categories: { network: \"allow\" } }, infers only one of them. Inferring the union would need a much more complex public signature. docs/MUTATION_INTEGRITY.md now says to annotate such a policy as SecretGuardPolicy<string | null>, which keeps both.\n- P3: the 90 s purity test override. Fix: split into three independent online scenarios (update available plus the offline sweep, up to date plus an offline override, registry unavailable), each with one registry-backed lookup and its own fixture and registry, all inside CI's default 30 s. The stalled-provider scenario was already separate."
  "2026-11-18T15:18:08.153Z","harness:claude-code","CI on 3f02776a1 failed two gates, both fixed:\n- Gates (static): the CodeFactor complexity ceiling of 16, with runBoundedUpdateQuery at 19. Fix: the provider teardown moved into createProviderTeardown, a helper with its own documented contract, and the unreachable re-entry guard is gone. The static quality gate now reports no complexity violations.\n- Gates (coverage): 99.99%. The default root's alive() in killProcessTree was never called, and update-check line 160's re-entry branch was dead. Fix: a unit test covers a successful ps without a caller root, and the dead branch was removed with the refactor.\nCodeRabbit's fifth pass (on 3f02776a1): 11 minor findings.\n- Records: pm-5kfzjh's resolution describes provider termination as best effort. pm-gh1749's contract_change states the precedence (exact own rules entry, then category, then default), and its actual result separates inherited keys from explicit own entries.\n- Docs: the PACKAGE_EVIDENCE table column is now \"Other provider execution failure\", with output-validation codes noted. The doc separates a lookup's own deadline from when manage returns, since queued lookups wait for a free provider slot outside --fix-managed-state.\n- Code: normalizing a stored timed-out record sets update_available to null. A provider still alive when the 2 s exit wait runs out is unref'd, so it cannot keep the host running.\n- Tests: the registry fixture rejects a denied bind; the live-tree test requires two distinct, valid PIDs; the ordering test keeps the ESRCH for an exited descendant.\n- Declined: a real-process test that the Windows ping descendant exits. taskkill /T is unit-tested with mocks, and the repository's real-process suite is POSIX-only."
  "2026-11-18T15:47:07.434Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 16 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 3ec43cf6a). Both findings fixed:\n- P2: descendant identity, raised for the fourth time across reviewers. Instead of declining again, the teardown now narrows the window. descendantEntriesFromProcessTable keeps each descendant's snapshot parent. Right before signalling, parentStillMatches re-reads /proc/<pid>/stat on Linux (the ppid is parsed after the command name's last closing parenthesis) and skips a PID whose parent changed. Descendants are killed deepest first, so a live parent's children are checked while that parent is still alive. A PID that vanished is skipped; without procfs (macOS, BSD) the snapshot is trusted, which is documented. killProcessTree is split into killWindowsTree and killPosixDescendants to stay under the complexity ceiling.\n- P2: a teardown helper (ps or taskkill) that cannot die after its 2 s deadline is now unref'd, so it cannot keep pm running, matching the provider teardown.\nTests cover command names with spaces and parentheses, reused and vanished PIDs with and without procfs, the default /proc read, a skipped reused descendant in killProcessTree, and the helper unref. The unit spec mocks readFileSync, so a fake PID never reads a real process's /proc entry."
  "2026-11-18T15:57:10.697Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 17 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 64341881a). Both findings fixed:\n- P2: in a nested PID namespace that inherits an outer /proc, ps and /proc list outer PIDs while process.kill interprets them in this namespace. Fix: processTableSharesNamespace requires /proc/self/stat to name this process (process.pid) before the descendant walk runs; otherwise only the root is killed through its handle. Without procfs there are no PID namespaces to confuse.\n- P3: a Windows worker thread gets a case-sensitive copy of process.env, so SYSTEMROOT or WINDIR were missed. Fix: resolveWindowsSystemRoot matches the variable names case-insensitively and keeps the fully-qualified-path check.\nUnit tests cover the namespace check (a matching pid, a foreign pid, vanished with procfs, no procfs, and the default read), the walk being skipped for a foreign namespace, and upper-case environment keys."
  "2026-11-18T16:07:13.893Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 18 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 60c930f86). Each finding and how it was handled:\n- P2: the /proc/self/stat PID check could pass in a nested namespace where the numeric PIDs happen to match. Fix: processTableSharesNamespace now requires namespace evidence, an NSpid line in /proc/self/status with a single entry equal to process.pid. More entries (an outer /proc), a different PID, or a kernel without NSpid all fall back to killing only the root through its handle. Unit tests cover each case; the real host's single NSpid entry keeps the real-process walk test meaningful.\n- P2: the pm process can outlive its reported result by up to about 4 s: a 2 s teardown helper, then a 2 s exit wait. Fix: documented in PACKAGE_EVIDENCE.md separately from SDK result delivery, which stays immediate.\n- P3: on a system without /bin/ps or /usr/bin/ps (NixOS), the descendant-kill integration assertion would fail against the documented best-effort fallback. Fix: that one test is skipped when no system ps exists; the deadline tests still run."
  "2026-11-18T16:16:38.056Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit's sixth pass (on 64341881a): 15 minor findings. 14 fixed, 1 declined.\n- Code: at the deadline the timeout is now delivered first. The teardown, whose first steps are synchronous process and filesystem checks (process.kill on the group, existsSync, /proc reads), starts in a zero-delay timer on a later turn of the event loop. Writing the tests for this exposed a real ordering bug: the overdue branch of settle (performance.now() at or past the deadline) built its error from a teardown variable that was not yet set, so the deferred teardown was lost and a provider slot was freed early. The deadline handler now records the deferred teardown before settling. A unit test pins that the result arrives before teardown starts.\n- Code: the managed-state normalizer trims update_error before recognising a timeout, as the extension summary does, for both the budget and the availability.\n- Records: pm-gh1749's contract says \"defined own entry\" (an undefined entry falls through). pm-43p2c3's contract and resolution, and pm-5kfzjh's contract, limit the failed codes to execution failures, since output-validation codes such as no_remote_reference_found and invalid_npm_registry_metadata stay separate. pm-9x9drr's actual result no longer counts teardown time under the lock. pm-gh1749's linked type test now runs pnpm build first, because the type tests resolve the package's dist declarations.\n- Docs: inheritance holds only without a defined own rules entry; immediate failure only for exits observed before the deadline; the SecretGuardPolicy JSDoc on unshipped rule keys.\n- Tests: the offline override provably makes no request; the ps probe is bounded (5 s, SIGKILL); the stderr fixture writes a marker proving it ran; non-timeout results use toStrictEqual.\n- Declined: on Windows with both SystemRoot and windir unset, the linked-test runner no longer finds taskkill on PATH. Resolving it through PATH or the working directory is exactly what the trusted-path change removed, and Windows always sets SystemRoot, so only a deliberately stripped SDK host environment loses tree teardown. Its root is still killed."
  "2026-11-18T16:30:16.878Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 19 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 2c4e68f94). Both findings fixed:\n- P2: the overflow and stdio-less paths still started the teardown before settling, so slow synchronous teardown setup could push an on-time overflow past the deadline and turn it into a timeout. Fix: one deferTeardown() helper serves every path (deadline, overflow, no stdout). It records the deferred teardown and starts it in a zero-delay timer, so every outcome is classified first. A test shows that a provider which exited before its deferred teardown started is never torn down by PID.\n- P3: the descendant-kill integration test also needs a process table from this PID namespace. Fix: it is skipped when processTableSharesNamespace() is false, as well as when no system ps exists."
  "2026-11-18T16:40:55.846Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 20 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on eaf053208): a single finding, test-only. In a nested PID namespace that inherits an outer /proc, the Ctrl-C integration test's ps-based liveness probe looks up inner PIDs in the outer table. Fix: that test is skipped when processTableSharesNamespace() is false, like the descendant-kill test. Codex found no product findings this round."
  "2026-11-18T16:48:21.716Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 21 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on ee6f4cf40): two findings, both declined with reasons. Codex reports convergence: round 20 found nothing in the product code.\n- P2: under the extension state lock, manage --fix-managed-state releases a provider permit when the lookup settles, so the next batch can start while timed-out providers are still being killed. That is the deliberate trade-off from round 15: the locked path must not take longer under the lock than on main. Every extra provider has already been sent SIGKILL, and its teardown helpers are bounded and unref'd. The real fix, refreshing after the lock is released so permits can be held through teardown, is pm-9x9drr's scope.\n- P3: PM_EXTENSION_UPDATE_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS is read case-sensitively in a Windows worker thread's env copy. pm reads every PM_* variable by its exact name (process.env.PM_...). The main-thread Windows environment is case-insensitive, and a worker's copy keeps the host's own spelling, so matching just this one variable case-insensitively would be an inconsistent special case."
  "2026-11-18T17:25:15.951Z","harness:claude-code","Final-head Codex review (on 65c97be7a), both findings fixed: (1) P2, the immutable close event of pm-5kfzjh still described the superseded design (detached providers with signal handlers, a 120 s cap, a 60 s test budget); pm-5kfzjh was reopened with item reopen and closed again with the shipped implementation and verification evidence. (2) P3, the GitHub overflow unit test now awaits updateCheckTeardown(status), so its deferred teardown can never run inside a later test after the mocks reset."
  "2026-11-18T17:39:44.677Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review of 27f7c3426: two P2 findings, both fixed.\n- A host that exits right after a timeout result, such as an SDK host calling process.exit(), could abandon the deferred teardown before any signal was sent, leaving npm or git itself running. Fix: while a teardown is pending, one shared exit listener (no signal handlers) synchronously kills each pending provider through its ChildProcess handle. The listener exists only while a teardown is pending, and a unit test asserts it is added once, kills the provider, and is removed. The pm CLI never calls process.exit, so its teardown always runs in full.\n- Earlier releases persisted a failed GitHub check as git's command line and stderr. The managed-state normalizer now reads any non-code GitHub update_error as github_remote_lookup_failed, so upgraded workspaces never surface that text (for example in package doctor --detail deep). Stable codes and npm reasons are unchanged. A unit test covers the legacy text, a stable code and an npm reason."
  "2026-11-18T17:49:07.980Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review of 7cf66460d: two findings, both fixed. (1) P2: a stored GitHub update_error that is blank has always meant no error (resolveUpdateCheckResolution trims it), but the legacy normalizer turned it into github_remote_lookup_failed, which could make package doctor --strict-exit fail. Blank values now pass through unchanged, and the unit test covers that case. (2) P3: tests/unit/extensions/npm-update-check.spec.ts, which this item's linked command runs and this PR changed, is now in the item's files."
  "2026-11-18T18:06:38.072Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review of 8ec6678d6: two findings, both fixed. (1) P2: checking only a descendant's immediate parent could be fooled when an intermediate PID was reused while ps listed the table. ancestryStillMatches now re-reads every link from the descendant up to the provider before the signal; unit tests cover a reused intermediate and a missing link. (2) P3: two fake-timer tests could leave a deferred teardown and its exit listener behind. The spec's afterEach now runs the pending fake timers before restoring real timers, and the spec also passes in shuffled order."
  "2026-11-18T18:20:43.628Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review of f4df00ef4: three findings, all fixed. (1) P2: on Linux an unreadable /proc/self/status counted as no procfs and the walk ran unverified. processTableSharesNamespace now fails closed on Linux (only a system without procfs needs no evidence), and only the root is killed there. (2) P3: the ancestry check reread every link for every descendant, which is quadratic for deep trees. A per-teardown verified map now reads each link once; a unit test with a 1,000-level chain asserts 1,000 reads. (3) P3: docs/RELEASING.md now says to install pm-changelog (pnpm changelog:pm:install) before recording contract_change on a fresh clone, because the field is registered by that package."
  "2026-11-18T18:37:33.249Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review of 7af1369db: one P2, fixed. The verified-link cache let an intermediate that was checked while verifying a deeper descendant be signalled from that cached result, even if it exited after its child was killed and its PID was reused. killPosixDescendants now rereads each signal target's own link immediately before its signal, and uses the cache only for the rest of the chain, which stays linear. A unit test kills the leaf, has its launcher's PID reused, and asserts that the replacement is not signalled."
  "2026-11-18T19:01:47.990Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review of 536c28739, two more findings, both fixed. (1) P2: on Windows, a worker environment without SystemRoot or windir fell back to killing only cmd.exe and left the linked test running. resolveWindowsSystemRoot now falls back to the fixed, fully qualified C:\\\\Windows when its System32 taskkill.exe exists; a qualified variable still wins, and relative or UNC values are still never used. Unit tests cover the fallback, the precedence and the missing-taskkill case. (2) P3: the integration probe counted a ps exit 1 with no row as proof of absence even if ps had rejected its options. It now also requires empty stderr and that process.kill(pid, 0) reports ESRCH. A new negative control spawns a sleep and asserts that waitUntilStopped reports it as still running, and as stopped only once it is killed. process-tree.ts stays at 100% coverage."
  "2026-11-18T19:13:01.100Z","harness:claude-code","Codex and Greptile on d60036798, P3: the new surviving-child control used the ps probe without the processTableSharesNamespace() guard that the other probe-based tests carry, so it failed in containers whose ps reads an outer /proc. It is now skipped there too."
  "2026-11-18T20:32:53.922Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit on 1dfb65b1a, two findings, both fixed. (1) process-tree: a read of an intermediate that failed while its child was checked was cached as false and still blocked the intermediate's own signal after a fresh read succeeded, leaving it running. Each target's own fresh read now decides on its own, and only the links above it come from the cache (ancestryStillMatches starts at the target's parent). The walk is reversed breadth-first, so every deeper descendant has been decided before its ancestor. A unit test with a failing first read asserts the child is skipped and the intermediate killed; the old code fails it. (2) docs/RELEASING.md: a fresh clone runs pnpm build before pnpm changelog:pm:install, which calls node dist/cli.js."
